1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,920
Rust is a systems language that
promises two things at once:

2
00:00:04,470 --> 00:00:07,600
the speed of C,
and no crashes from memory bugs.

3
00:00:08,717 --> 00:00:10,827
Most languages make you pick one.

4
00:00:11,052 --> 00:00:14,262
Garbage-collected languages are safe,
but they pause.

5
00:00:14,692 --> 00:00:17,842
Manual memory languages are fast,
but dangerous.

6
00:00:18,272 --> 00:00:19,862
Rust takes a third road.

7
00:00:20,883 --> 00:00:22,553
The core idea is ownership.

8
00:00:22,958 --> 00:00:25,268
Every value has exactly one owner.

9
00:00:25,593 --> 00:00:28,983
When that owner goes out of scope,
the value is freed automatically.

10
00:00:30,317 --> 00:00:32,247
You can lend a value out with a reference.

11
00:00:32,692 --> 00:00:34,842
Any number of readers can borrow it.

12
00:00:35,007 --> 00:00:37,397
Or one writer can borrow it mutably.

13
00:00:37,722 --> 00:00:39,592
Never both at the same time.

14
00:00:40,417 --> 00:00:43,507
Rust does not have null,
and does not have exceptions.

15
00:00:43,967 --> 00:00:45,977
Option means a value might be missing.

16
00:00:46,292 --> 00:00:48,722
Result means an operation might fail.

17
00:00:49,002 --> 00:00:50,952
The compiler makes you handle both.

18
00:00:52,017 --> 00:00:54,807
Cargo is the build tool
and package manager.

19
00:00:55,192 --> 00:00:59,022
It compiles, tests, formats and publishes,
all with one command.

20
00:01:00,183 --> 00:01:01,933
Fast, safe and predictable.

21
00:01:02,373 --> 00:01:04,463
All of it without a garbage collector.

